Close the mkdirat(2)/mknodat(2) existence oracle.

  mkdirat(AT_FDCWD, p, 0700)
  mknodat(AT_FDCWD, p, S_IFIFO|0600, 0)

MEASURED on OP15 (Android 16, 6.12.23, engine v26, Suite v1.3.60) as hidden uid
10422 against /product/etc/permissions, with TWO controls -- an absent sibling
and a stock-VISIBLE sibling -- and repeated three times:

  hidden injected path   -> EEXIST
  genuinely absent path  -> EROFS
  stock visible file     -> EEXIST

So a hidden path answers exactly like an ordinary existing file AND differs from
an absent one. That is the strongest shape a tell can take: a scanner needs one
mkdirat() and no control path at all.

The probed path was the single entry in the live _ghost table at the time, so
this is a HOLE IN THE CLOAK, not merely an unenumerated neighbour.

A THIRD ORDERING

The two mechanisms this directory already documents do not explain it:

  1. the syscall never reaches a file-level op (O_PATH, *xattr, ENOTDIR, link)
  2. mnt_want_write() answers first (truncate, utimensat, chmod)

This is neither. filename_create() calls mnt_want_write() EARLY and deliberately
keeps going -- "don't fail immediately if it's r/o, at least try to report other
errors" -- stashing the result in err2. It then does the exclusive lookup, and a
POSITIVE dentry returns -EEXIST before err2 is ever consulted. A negative dentry
falls through to `if (unlikely(err2))` and reports -EROFS. Existence wins over
the write check, which is the opposite order from truncate.

WHY THE FIX IS NOT -ENOENT

Every other guard in this directory returns -ENOENT, because for those syscalls
that is what an absent path reports. Here it is NOT: an absent name reports
EROFS instead. Returning -ENOENT would leave the path still distinguishable
from an absent one, merely inverted -- a new oracle in place of the old. The
guard has to report err2, the very error the absent path would have reached.

WHERE THE GUARD GOES, AND WHY THERE

Inside the `d_is_positive(dentry)` arm, after the lookup and before `goto fail`.
That is AFTER the parent's search permission has been enforced, so a caller who
cannot search the directory still gets -EACCES exactly as it would for an absent
name, and it is on the path that ALREADY refuses to create anything -- the arm
ends in `goto fail` either way, so this can never turn into a create.

`err2 &&` is tested first, cheapest-first like ghost_hidden_path()'s own gates:
no path is rendered on a writable mount. On a writable mount an absent name
would be CREATED here, and that cannot be emulated without clobbering the file
being hidden, so -EEXIST stands -- no worse than an unpatched kernel.

filename_create() is shared by mkdirat, mknodat, symlinkat and linkat's target,
so one guard covers all four. do_renameat2() does its own lookup and is NOT
routed through here -- measured clean (hidden EROFS, absent EROFS), because
there mnt_want_write does win.

APPLIES TO

The `error = -EEXIST;` / `d_is_positive(dentry)` block in filename_create() is
byte-identical on all five fleet kernel versions (v5.10, v5.15, v6.1, v6.6,
v6.12) even though the lookup ABOVE it is not -- 5.10 calls __lookup_hash(),
6.12 calls lookup_one_qstr_excl(), and 6.12 splits the call across two lines.
That is why the context below starts at `goto unlock;` and not at the lookup:
three lines of context is the most that is common to all five.

`error = -EEXIST;` appears TWICE in every one of these files. The second is in
do_renameat2(), where it reads `if ((flags & RENAME_NOREPLACE) &&
d_is_positive(new_dentry))` and has NO blank line above it. The blank line in
the context below is what pins this hunk to filename_create(); the trailing
`Special case` comment pins it again. Do not shorten either end.

DO NOT HAND-AUTHOR THE HUNK

Generate it: apply the change to a real tree and `diff -u`. A hand-written hunk
here had four leading context lines and three trailing -- self-consistent counts,
byte-identical context, correct start line -- and `patch -F0` refused it on all
five trees while `git apply` took it silently. Regenerating with symmetric
context fixed it; -U3 and -U4 both apply, the asymmetric one does not.

Verify with `patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run`, never with `git apply --check`: git is the
more permissive of the two, and the builders use patch.

Dry-run at -F0 against v5.10, v5.15, v6.1, v6.6 and v6.12: applies to all five,
landing inside filename_create() on each (offsets -420, -191, -151, -144, 0).

